WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.400
Act 1, years. A flat, very wide map of the world at night: two copies of the map side by side, land in dark grey, cities as yellow-white dots. The year label in the middle runs from 2012 to 2023, one year about every half second. Each year is one quick left-to-right sweep of soft plucks: left is west, right is east, higher notes are further north, louder is brighter.

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:09.600
2023. The lights of the eastern United States, Europe, the Nile, India and eastern China are the brightest. Bells are now ringing on top of the plucks: they mark places that are brighter than in 2012, and there are more of them every year.

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:13.800
Act 2, change map. The lights fade and the map shows only the change between 2012 and 2023. A colour bar appears: purple for more light, orange for less. First sweep: only the purple, as high bells, scattered most of all across Asia, Africa and the Middle East.

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:17.400
Second sweep of the same picture: only the orange, as low plucks, where the lights went out or dimmed, for example in parts of Europe.

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:21.000
Third sweep: purple and orange together, at their true balance.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:23.000
Act 3, regional zooms. The night-lights map returns, back to 2012. Four zoom boxes pop out of the map: Western Europe, the Eastern Mediterranean, Ukraine and India.

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:27.700
A tour of the four boxes, left to right, each with its own instrument and side of the stereo field: Western Europe, the Eastern Mediterranean with the bright line of the Nile, Ukraine, then India.

00:00:27.700 --> 00:00:36.800
The years run again, 2013 to 2023, inside all four boxes at once. Each year all four regions sweep together; bells an octave up mark places that are brighter than in 2012. India and the Eastern Mediterranean grow steadily brighter; Western Europe stays about the same.

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:43.333
Final change map inside the boxes, purple for more light, orange for less, toured box by box: Western Europe mostly orange, the Eastern Mediterranean and Ukraine a mix of both, India almost all purple. Purple plays as high bells, orange as low plucks.
